<!--
    GH-881 part B, AArch64 control for option calleereadarg: a register a
    callee only stores is no evidence of an argument where the convention has
    no caller-side variadic marker.

        int openish(const char *p, int flags, ...);

    gcc bounds a variadic callee's register save to the va_arg it can reach,
    so openish only stores x2 to its frame (str x2, [sp, #24]) and reads it
    back when flags has bit 6.  x86-64 callers mark a variadic call with
    xor %eax,%eax; AArch64 callers mark nothing, so a value a caller happens
    to hold in w2 looks like a third argument.

      caller   tests z = *q in w2 and tail-calls openish(p, 0): openish(a0,0)
               in both passes, where the store would have added w2.
      caller2  is openish(p, 0x40, m) with m tested in w2: the third argument
               is real but only stored, so it stays out in both passes too.
               The rule gives up this case to refuse the first.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 active input-trial scoring, option calleereadarg
    off|on.  Both passes print the same calls.
-->
